## Paper 1: NeurIPS (AI Safety & Alignment Track)
Title: Quantifying Semantic Drift and Alignment Trust Signals in Iterative Agentic Core Data Systems
## Section 1: Introduction
Autonomous multi-agent systems are rapidly transitioning from conversational playthings to the analytical backbone of enterprise data infrastructure. In these ecosystems, specialized large language model (LLM) agents sequentially manipulate, reason over, and pass data states to downstream nodes to solve multi-step analytical objectives. While single-turn LLM alignment has seen rigorous optimization via methods like Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF), multi-agent loops introduce a compounding, non-linear failure mode: semantic drift.
As stochastic outputs from one agent serve as the context or constraint for the next, subtle misalignments propagate through the system execution trajectory. This results in catastrophic drift where the final system state diverges entirely from the user's core constraints, all while masquerading as a valid execution path. Traditional evaluation metrics, such as BLEU, ROUGE, or static LLM-as-a-judge frameworks, are unsuited for this dynamic because they evaluate text overlap or isolated checkpoints rather than tracking the entropy of a continuous state space over time.
In this work, we formalize this phenomenon by introducing a mathematical framework to quantify semantic drift within multi-agent networks. Our main contributions are:

   1. We derive a novel metric, the Semantic Trust Index ($\mathcal{ST_I}$), based on tracking latent space token vector divergence across multi-turn trajectories.
   2. We demonstrate a predictable phase transition in multi-agent depth where information entropy overrides structural steering constraints.
   3. We introduce an automated semantic anchoring algorithm that dynamically recalibrates agent trajectories, reducing semantic drift by up to 40% across long-running pipelines without internal model weight updates.

[Agent 1 Output] ---> (Latent Vector v1) 
                            \ 
                             ---> [Agent 2 Process] ---> (Latent Vector v2) ===> Drift Delta_s (\mathcal{ST_I})
                            /
[Anchoring Node] -----------> (Dynamic Recalibration Constraint)

## Experimental Setup & Dataset Criteria

* Base Models: Evaluate using open-weight architectures (Llama-3-70B, Mixtral-8x22B) and proprietary foundation endpoints (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet) via standard orchestration layers (LangChain/LangGraph).
* Evaluation Dataset:
* Public Baseline: AgentBench or BIRD-SQL execution trajectories, isolating tasks requiring $\geq 5$ consecutive agent handoffs.
   * Synthetic Scale Up: Utilize [MSR's SynthLLM framework](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/articles/synthllm-breaking-the-ai-data-wall-with-scalable-synthetic-data/) to generate 10,000 multi-turn data engineering interaction logs.
* Dataset Criteria: Text/code trajectories must include ground-truth programmatic specifications at step zero. The dataset must map distinct metadata tiers (Technical, Semantic, and Operational) across sequential agent actions to verify where information decay occurs.

