## Paper 3: ICLR (Representation Learning & Prompt Engineering)
Title: Evaluating Hierarchical Abstraction Layers for Precise Semantic Multi-Step Prompting in Complex Enterprise Tools
## Section 1: Introduction
When executing complex tasks over multi-step tool networks, the reasoning capacity of Transformer-based models degrades as a function of action-space dimensionality. As observed by Turing Award winner Edsger W. Dijkstra, "The purpose of abstraction is not to be vague, but to create a new semantic level in which one can be absolutely precise." Current prompt engineering paradigms largely ignore this principle, relying instead on flat, verbose instructions that force the model's internal attention mechanisms to compute relationships across technical and business variables simultaneously.
Consequently, hidden token representations collapse under high-context cognitive load, leading to improper tool parameter mapping and broken execution chains.
In this work, we formalize Hierarchical Abstraction Prompting (HAP), a representation framework that introduces strict semantic layering over the input domain space. By explicitly partitioning an LLM’s input prompt into independent, decoupled conceptual tiers—separating structural data definitions from abstract operational intents—we fundamentally reshape the attention map topology of the underlying transformer.
We prove that by structuring prompts through strict semantic abstraction boundaries, models maintain sharp internal representations of the action space, allowing them to solve deep multi-turn tool reasoning problems that cause un-abstracted baselines to hallucinate or stall.

[Standard Flat Prompting] 
Prompt Context ---> [ High-Dimensional Token Space ] ---> Attention Blur / Collapse

[Hierarchical Abstraction Prompting (HAP)]
Prompt Context ---> [ Tier 1: Abstract Business Ontology ] ---\ 
               ---> [ Tier 2: Structural Data Schema    ] ---> [ Localized, Precise Attention Map ]

## Experimental Setup & Dataset Criteria

* Analysis Method: Utilize probing classifiers and attention map visualization tools (such as a modified version of [Microsoft's open-source SandDance](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/sanddance/) layout engine) to measure attention weight clustering inside the final layers of a 8B and 70B parameter model.
* Evaluation Dataset:
* Public Baseline: ToolBench or GAIA (General AI Assistants) benchmark suite, filtering specifically for enterprise tool utilization tasks (file operations, API orchestration, and multi-join querying).
* Dataset Criteria: Test instances must require a minimum of 4 distinct sequential API or tool evaluations. The tools must feature nested JSON parameters where the outer object defines a business constraint and the inner object maps to an explicit data type system.

