## Paper 4: CVPR / ICCV (Multimodal Vision-Language Track)
Title: Mitigating Visual Hallucination in Multi-Agent VLM Interfaces for Enterprise Analytics
## Section 1: Introduction
The rapid advancement of Multimodal Vision-Language Models (VLMs) has spurred their integration into business intelligence pipelines, where they act as intuitive user interfaces for data visualization, dashboard comprehension, and complex chart analysis. Despite their high perceptual capabilities, state-of-the-art VLMs suffer from a critical flaw when deployed in enterprise analytics settings: cross-modal visual hallucination.
Unlike standard text hallucinations, visual hallucinations occur when a VLM misinterprets spatial chart geometries, misaligns multi-axis trends, or completely fabricates data values from visual noise, subsequently passing corrupted logical premises to downstream code generators or decision agents. In business reporting applications, a single misread data point on an analytical chart can cause automated systems to execute highly flawed database commands.
To solve this vulnerability, we introduce the Dual-Route Vision Auditor (DRVA), a multi-agent computer vision framework designed to secure and validate multimodal analytical tasks. DRVA splits VLM inference into two isolated processing trajectories: a primary visual perception route and a programmatic schema cross-validation route. By implementing an automated, cross-modal verification matrix that cross-checks visual chart interpretations directly with backend database queries, our system identifies and suppresses visual hallucination vectors before they reach downstream execution steps.
## Experimental Setup & Dataset Criteria

* Models: Evaluate leading VLMs including GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, and open multimodal architectures like LLaVA-NeXT or CogVLM.
* Evaluation Dataset:
* Public Baseline: ChartQA, PlotQA, and OpenComplexQA benchmarks.
   * Curated Benchmark Subset: Collaborate with the Brandeis Visual Analytics Laboratory (BraVA) to compile a specialized validation set of 2,000 highly deceptive, complex enterprise dashboard frames featuring dual-axis configurations, stacked bar distributions, and highly condensed legends.
* Dataset Criteria: Every image instance in the evaluation corpus must be accompanied by its raw, ground-truth tabular backing dataset (e.g., CSV, JSON data frames) along with a set of analytical reasoning questions that test precise quantitative values rather than generic visual summaries.

